From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 17:50:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] glifo: shrink a bitmap glyph once, to the pixels it covers

A colour emoji's strike is usually far larger than the text it sits in:
Noto Color Emoji's is 136 pixels, drawn at 20 to 40. Drawn from the strike
with a bilinear image paint, each pixel samples only the 2x2 strike pixels
around its centre, so at a shrink of five most of the strike is skipped
and the glyph's round edges stair-step, next to FreeType-based renderers
and browsers, which shrink the bitmap first.

When a bitmap glyph's transform shrinks it on both axes, without skew,
rotation or mirroring, shrink it once to the pixels it covers, each the
average of the strike over the area it covers, in premultiplied
components. Then draw that copy one to one at the strike's position
rounded to whole pixels, so it is not resampled again; colour bitmap
glyphs are not drawn at sub-pixel positions in browsers either. The atlas
caches the copy under its size rather than the strike's ppem: a bitmap
drawn at or above its strike's size keeps its key, and a shrunk one's size
can never equal a ppem.

The shrink is separable and in fixed point, with a pixel's four
components in one u64 in the columns' pass. For Noto Color Emoji's strike
it costs about half of what decoding the strike's PNG does, which glifo
does for every bitmap glyph it does not find in the atlas.
